  "TITLE": "Fatigue Failure:<br>Breaking Below the<br>Rated Strength",
  "LEAD": "Run a material through enough stress cycles and it can crack and fail at a stress far below its rated tensile strength &mdash; that's fatigue, and the S-N curve (stress amplitude vs. cycles to failure) is how engineers quantify it. Steel has an endurance limit: stay below that stress and the part can survive effectively forever. Aluminum never levels off &mdash; every stress level eventually fails if cycled enough, so the only design lever left is how many cycles it will actually see, which is why vibrating or thermally cycled plant equipment gets inspected on a schedule tied to cumulative cycles, not a one-time strength check.",
